わっぱ。よう、わっぱ。よう、わっぱ。
Kiddo. Hey, kiddo. Hey, kiddo.

263
00:26:22,140 --> 00:26:26,090
Hello! Can you help me write a report for school?

264
00:26:26,690 --> 00:26:32,340
ハロー、学校の宿題手伝ってくれない？
Hello, can you help me with my school homework?

265
00:26:33,190 --> 00:26:34,740
Oh, yes!

266
00:26:35,090 --> 00:26:36,740
いいわよ。
Sure thing.

267
00:26:36,890 --> 00:26:38,590
ルーシーおばあさん、イエス。
Grandma Lucy says yes.

268
00:26:38,690 --> 00:26:40,040
いいわよ。
Sure thing.

269
00:26:40,290 --> 00:26:44,040
I am a great writer. I have a very popular blog.

270
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:46,140
ブリティッシュイングリッシュじゃん。
That's British English.

271
00:26:46,440 --> 00:26:47,540
もう1回聞くか。
Let's listen again.

272
00:26:47,590 --> 00:26:51,440
I am a great writer. I have a very popular blog.

273
00:26:51,640 --> 00:27:00,040
ブログ。あ、私は超有名なブログ書くすごいライターなのよ。
Blog. Oh, I'm an amazing writer who writes a super famous blog.

274
00:27:00,840 --> 00:27:05,390
ブログ書くの得意？え、じゃあわしも戦えるかも。
Good at writing blogs? Huh, then maybe I can compete too.

275
00:27:05,490 --> 00:27:08,090
わしもかつてブログを書いていた時期があるから。
Because I also had a period where I wrote a blog.

276
00:27:08,190 --> 00:27:13,490
わしもかつてはてなブログとかをやっていた時期があるから、ちょっと気持ちわかるかも。
I used to use Hatena Blog and stuff back then, so I kind of get how she feels.

277
00:27:15,340 --> 00:27:19,290
え、なんかブリティッシュじゃない？ブリティッシュっぽくない？発音。
Wait, isn't that British? Doesn't her pronunciation sound British?

278
00:27:19,990 --> 00:27:22,440
Do you read my blog?

279
00:27:22,540 --> 00:27:25,190
Do you read my blog?

280
00:27:25,290 --> 00:27:28,540
子供に何でも読ませてんな！子供に何でも読ませてんな！
She's making a kid read whatever! She's making a kid read anything!

281
00:27:28,940 --> 00:27:34,540
私のブログを読みたーいかしらー？
Do you want to read my blog~?

282
00:27:35,990 --> 00:27:39,890
子供が読んでいいブログか？子供が読んでいいブログなのか？
Is it a blog okay for kids to read? Is it really appropriate for kids?

283
00:27:42,240 --> 00:27:46,840
Do you read、あ、読んでますかの方？読んでますかの方なのかな？
'Do you read'... Oh, is it 'do you read it'? Does it mean 'are you reading it'?

284
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:54,190
読みたいかしらんじゃない？Do you readだから、読む？読むか…読む？か。
It's not 'do you want to read', right? Since it's 'Do you read', it's 'Do you read it?'... Yeah, 'Do you read it?'.

285
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:55,840
No.

286
00:27:55,890 --> 00:27:56,790
No.

287
00:27:56,990 --> 00:27:58,540
読んでませんか。
You don't read it?

288
00:27:59,140 --> 00:28:04,290
読んだことある？読んだ？読んでない。読むかい？読まない。
Have you ever read it? Did you read it? I don't read it. Do you read it? No.

289
00:28:04,940 --> 00:28:06,890
I have a lot of books.

290
00:28:07,190 --> 00:28:09,240
たくさんの本を持っているわ。
I have a lot of books.

291
00:28:10,390 --> 00:28:13,440
Do you need my books for research?

292
00:28:14,040 --> 00:28:27,590
Do you need my books for... あー、ブログ作戦失敗したから、本いっぱいあるよ、本いっぱいあるけど、あのー、宿題用に使う？
Do you need my books for... Ah, since the blog plan failed, she's like, 'I have lots of books, do you want to use them for your homework?'

293
00:28:28,290 --> 00:28:28,940
うん。
Yeah.

294
00:28:29,990 --> 00:28:32,340
I don't need your books.

295
00:28:33,190 --> 00:28:37,590
なんか振られまくりじゃないルーシー？ルーシー断られまくりじゃない？
Isn't Lucy getting rejected left and right? Isn't she getting turned down constantly?

296
00:28:37,840 --> 00:28:42,890
I don't need your books. 別にいらんのじゃ。
I don't need your books. I don't need them.

297
00:28:43,490 --> 00:28:45,490
What is your report about?

298
00:28:45,690 --> 00:28:49,090
あなたは何についてのレポートを書いてるの？
What is your report about?

299
00:28:49,890 --> 00:28:54,540
皆さんも本持ってる？そんなには持ってないね。あんま本読まないのじゃ。
Do you all have books? I don't have that many. I don't really read books.

300
00:28:55,490 --> 00:28:56,440
Dinosaurs.

301
00:28:56,590 --> 00:29:01,390
恐竜！あ、非常に、非常に子供らしい。そうじゃよね。
Dinosaurs! Oh, that's very, very kid-like. Right, right.

302
00:29:02,090 --> 00:29:07,740
別に恐竜の本とかあんまルーシー読まなそうだもんな。恐竜の本が読みたいのじゃ。
Lucy doesn't really seem like she'd read dinosaur books anyway. He wants to read books about dinosaurs.

303
00:29:08,190 --> 00:29:10,740
Why do you want to talk to me?

304
00:29:11,040 --> 00:29:15,240
なんで…なんでわしにじゃあ話しかけたん？
Why... why did you talk to me then?

305
00:29:16,040 --> 00:29:18,340
Why do you want to talk to me?

306
00:29:18,490 --> 00:29:24,890
何について話してほしいか。Why do you want to talk to me? Why？でもなぜ、なぜだよ。
What do you want me to talk about... Why do you want to talk to me? 'Why?' means 'why', right?

307
00:29:25,140 --> 00:29:28,540
Why do you want to talk to me?

308
00:29:29,990 --> 00:29:33,540
なんで私に喋ってほしかったのか？
Why did you want me to talk to you?

309
00:29:36,290 --> 00:29:43,290
ん？Why？ Why do you want to talk to me? なんで私に話しかけたの？
Huh? Why? Why do you want to talk to me? Why did you come talk to me?

310
00:29:43,790 --> 00:29:47,740
は？なんで恐竜なのに私に話しかけたの？なんで？
Huh? Why did you talk to me if it's about dinosaurs? Why?

311
00:29:48,390 --> 00:29:49,040
次。
Next.

312
00:29:49,690 --> 00:29:51,840
Because you are very old.

313
00:29:55,740 --> 00:30:00,040
そういうことか！なんで、なんで恐竜について私に…
Oh, I get it! 'Why, why about dinosaurs to me...'

314
00:30:00,048 --> 00:30:05,048
……かけたんですか、つったら、「Because you are very old.」
...and when asked why, she said, 'Because you are very old.'

315
00:30:06,848 --> 00:30:11,698
だって、だって、おばあちゃんだから恐竜見たかなと思って。
Because, because I thought since you're a grandma, maybe you saw dinosaurs!

316
00:30:11,748 --> 00:30:15,348
そこまで、そこまでじゃないよ！
I'm not that, not THAT old!

317
00:30:15,448 --> 00:30:18,498
rude だよ！rude！失礼だよ！
That's rude! Rude! How disrespectful!

318
00:30:18,948 --> 00:30:21,198
なんだ？喧嘩売ってんのか？
What's that? Are you picking a fight with me?

319
00:30:21,748 --> 00:30:30,298
いやー、お年寄りだから恐竜も詳しいかなと思って。おばあちゃんが子供の頃は恐竜いたんでしょ？
Well, I thought since you're elderly, maybe you'd know a lot about dinosaurs. Dinosaurs were around when you were a kid, Grandma, right?

320
00:30:32,948 --> 00:30:36,648
めらが子供の時も恐竜いないけどな、別に。
There weren't any dinosaurs when I was a kid either, though.

321
00:30:37,698 --> 00:30:45,648
When I am young でも I was young でも no dinosaurs だけどな。わしもいない。
Whether it's 'when I am young' or 'I was young,' there were no dinosaurs. Not for me either.

322
00:30:46,348 --> 00:30:54,598
Mela, can you tell us about dinosaurs? No, I don't know! I never see dinosaurs.
Mela, can you tell us about dinosaurs? No, I don't know! I've never seen dinosaurs.

323
00:30:55,198 --> 00:31:04,548
なんか恐竜の化石、化石？なんだ、骨、骨みたいなさ、博物館みたいなのはさ、見たけど、それ以外は知らないのじゃ。
Like, dinosaur fossils—fossils? What is it, like bones—I've seen them in places like museums, but other than that, I don't know-noja.

324
00:31:07,398 --> 00:31:14,298
Salamanders dinosaurs? No, サラマンダーはダイナソーじゃないよ。Salamanders not dinosaurs.
Salamanders are dinosaurs? No, salamanders aren't dinosaurs. Salamanders are not dinosaurs.

325
00:31:14,748 --> 00:31:20,498
だって恐竜はいたじゃろ？サラマンダーの方が架空なのじゃ。どっちかというとね。
Because dinosaurs actually existed, right? Salamanders are the fictional ones-noja, relatively speaking.

326
00:31:20,848 --> 00:31:26,148
サラマンダー、恐竜は火吹けないじゃろ。サラマンダーは火吹けるのじゃ。から違う。
Salamanders... dinosaurs can't breathe fire, right? Salamanders can breathe fire-noja. So they're different.

327
00:31:27,998 --> 00:31:29,898
鳥類じゃないよ、わし。
I'm not a bird, you know.

328
00:31:30,398 --> 00:31:38,248
Yes, dinosaurs birds. そう、恐竜は鳥じゃけど、わし鳥じゃないのじゃ。I am not bird なのじゃ。
Yes, dinosaurs are birds. Right, dinosaurs are birds, but I'm not a bird-noja. I am not a bird-noja.

329
00:31:39,998 --> 00:31:42,948
You know a lot of dinosaurs, right?

330
00:31:43,048 --> 00:31:46,848
たくさんの恐竜知ってるでしょ？ね、おばあちゃん。
You know a lot of dinosaurs, right? Hey, Grandma.

331
00:31:46,948 --> 00:31:48,248
Goodbye, Junior.

332
00:31:48,348 --> 00:31:49,948
Goodbye, Junior!

333
00:31:50,048 --> 00:31:51,548
Goodbye, Junior.

334
00:31:52,748 --> 00:31:54,548
バイバイ。バイバイ。
Bye-bye. Bye-bye.

335
00:31:54,748 --> 00:31:59,598
もういい、もうええわ、ありがとうございました！もうええわ、ありがとうございました！
That's enough, I'm done, thank you very much! That's enough, thank you very much!

336
00:32:02,048 --> 00:32:04,598
なるほどね、なるほどね、かわいそうに。
I see, I see, poor thing.

337
00:32:04,848 --> 00:32:16,548
たくさんの a lot of、でしょ right、必要ありません we don't need、調べ物 research、開けます opens。
Takusan no is 'a lot of', desho is 'right', hitsuyou arimasen is 'we don't need', shirabemono is 'research', akemasu is 'opens'.

338
00:32:16,598 --> 00:32:23,048
怒ってどっか行っちゃった。小童相手に、小童相手に怒って「もういい！もういいのじゃ！さらばじゃ！」って行っちゃった。
She got mad and walked off. Getting mad at a little kid, getting mad at a brat and leaving like, 'I'm done! That's enough-noja! Farewell-ja!'

339
00:32:24,348 --> 00:32:32,348
Ah, salamanders and dinosaurs friends Mela? No, I never, I never see dinosaurs.
Ah, are salamanders and dinosaurs friends, Mela? No, I've never, I've never seen dinosaurs.

340
00:32:33,348 --> 00:32:36,648
right、right をもっと使えるようになりたいのじゃ。
Right, I want to be able to use 'right' more-noja.

341
00:32:38,248 --> 00:32:41,598
かわいそうにおばあちゃん。そんな古くないよね。
Poor Grandma. She's not that old, right?

342
00:32:41,948 --> 00:32:51,548
大人げない？ま、確かに大人げなくはある。大人げなくはあるが、まあコンディションというものがね人間はありますからね。イライラしやすい日というのもありますからね。
Immature? Well, it definitely is immature. It is immature, but humans have their off days, you know. There are days when you get irritated easily.

343
00:32:52,148 --> 00:33:00,298
あの、普段は流せることもイライラしやすい日じゃと流せないみたいなことも往々にして起こり得るので、そういう日だったのでしょう彼女も。
Like, things you can normally brush off, on an irritable day you might not be able to let slide—that happens all the time, so maybe she was just having one of those days too.

344
00:33:02,048 --> 00:33:09,048
次！ネクスト！わしは別にいいよ。わしはおばあちゃん扱いされてもいいよ。
Next! Next! I don't mind, personally. I don't mind being treated like a grandma.

345
00:33:09,148 --> 00:33:23,448
I'm okay if you guys say me grandma でもいいよ。なぜならわしは本当にお主らより長生きしてるからね。
'I'm okay if you guys call me grandma' is fine too. Because I've actually lived way longer than all of you, you know.

346
00:33:27,048 --> 00:33:38,348
いいよ、別にわしは。わし grandma、grandma のじゃ？お子様扱い？まあそれもいいよ。好きなようにするといいよ。
It's fine with me. Am I grandma, grandma-noja? Being treated like a kid? Well, that's fine too. Do whatever you like.

347
00:33:41,248 --> 00:33:46,348
イェス、grandma のじゃ。イェス、grandma のじゃ。イェス。
Yes, grandma-noja. Yes, grandma-noja. Yes.

348
00:33:46,548 --> 00:33:58,298
grandma でも、あの、キッズでもいいよ。何でもいいよ。好きに消費するといいのじゃ。
Grandma or, uh, kid is fine too. Whatever works. Feel free to perceive me however you like-noja.

349
00:33:59,048 --> 00:34:08,598
お姫様でもいいよ。プリンセスでもいいよ。Princess Mela is great too. 何でもいいよ。
Princess is fine too. Princess works. Princess Mela is great too. Anything is fine.

350
00:34:11,648 --> 00:34:14,648
好きなように。のじゃ。
However you like. Noja.

351
00:34:15,248 --> 00:34:24,798
次行くか。えっと、リスニングかな？リスニングなら一旦BGM切ってみるか。
Shall we move on? Um, is this listening? If it's listening, let's turn off the BGM for a bit.

352
00:34:25,048 --> 00:34:28,548
よし！スタート！リスニング？
Alright! Start! Listening?

353
00:34:30,248 --> 00:34:32,348
あ、ラジオ来た！レイディオ！
Oh, it's the radio! Radio!

354
00:34:32,348 --> 00:34:39,248
フォルスタッフの人間入門の時間へようこそ！このコーナーでは熊の僕が人間の世界の不思議を究明していくよ。
Welcome to Falstaff's Guide to Humans! In this segment, I, a bear, will investigate the mysteries of the human world.

355
00:34:33,648 --> 00:34:35,298
フォルスタッフの人間入門。
Falstaff's Guide to Humans.

356
00:34:38,148 --> 00:34:41,048
ヘーイ、フォルスタッフ！
Hey, Falstaff!

357
00:34:43,848 --> 00:34:47,148
始める前に、次の単語を覚えておこう。
Before we start, let's learn the following words.

358
00:34:47,248 --> 00:34:49,898
うん。単語教えてくれるよー。
Yeah. He's going to teach us vocabulary.

359
00:34:50,148 --> 00:34:51,048
mail

360
00:34:51,148 --> 00:34:53,098
mail、郵便かな？
Mail, is that yuubin?

361
00:34:54,148 --> 00:34:54,948
later

362
00:34:55,048 --> 00:34:56,448
あとで。
Atode.

363
00:34:56,948 --> 00:34:57,948
afternoon

364
00:34:58,048 --> 00:34:59,598
午後。
Gogo.

365
00:35:00,516 --> 00:35:01,056
Counter.

366
00:35:01,356 --> 00:35:02,456
カウンター。
Counter.

367
00:35:03,806 --> 00:35:05,106
わしの天敵。
My natural enemy.

368
00:35:06,156 --> 00:35:11,356
フォルスタッフです。今回は、頼み事をしている2人を盗み聞きしてきたよ。
I'm Falstaff. This time, I eavesdropped on two people asking for favors.

369
00:35:12,456 --> 00:35:14,756
Hi John, could you water my plants?

370
00:35:15,156 --> 00:35:17,256
Of course, these flowers need water.

371
00:35:15,356 --> 00:35:17,206
水やってくんない？いいよ。
"Could you water them?" "Sure."

372
00:35:17,906 --> 00:35:21,556
Thank you, and can you check the mail every day?

373
00:35:18,656 --> 00:35:20,456
花は水を必要としてる。
The flowers need water.

374
00:35:20,656 --> 00:35:22,756
郵便を毎日確認してくれる？
Can you check the mail every day?

375
00:35:22,456 --> 00:35:25,156
Thank you, and can you check the mail every day?

376
00:35:25,156 --> 00:35:27,056
うん、頼んでた。
Yeah, she asked him to.

377
00:35:28,506 --> 00:35:31,556
No problem. Can I put the mail on the counter?

378
00:35:29,356 --> 00:35:29,906
で、
And then,

379
00:35:30,306 --> 00:35:32,856
カウンターに郵便物を置いてもいいですか？
Can I put the mail on the counter?

380
00:35:33,306 --> 00:35:35,156
Can I put the mail on the counter?

381
00:35:35,356 --> 00:35:41,256
Can I put the... the mail... the mail on the counter?

382
00:35:41,856 --> 00:35:42,756
にょじゃ。
Nyoja.

383
00:35:44,056 --> 00:35:46,256
Yes, please put it on the counter.

384
00:35:44,856 --> 00:35:46,856
いいぞ。カウンターに置いていい。
Sure. You can put it on the counter.

385
00:35:46,656 --> 00:35:48,556
Don't worry, no problem.

386
00:35:47,556 --> 00:35:48,756
イージー。
Easy.

387
00:35:48,856 --> 00:35:51,456
Thank you, John. Call me in the afternoon.

388
00:35:49,556 --> 00:35:51,056
ありがとう、ジョン。
Thank you, John.

389
00:35:51,656 --> 00:35:53,456
No problem, see you later.

390
00:35:51,956 --> 00:35:54,356
午後呼んで。またね。
Call me in the afternoon. See you later.

391
00:35:56,056 --> 00:35:59,656
彼女が彼に頼んだのは... 午後に電話すること。
What she asked him to do is... call her in the afternoon.

392
00:36:02,056 --> 00:36:06,156
他の人間に頼み事をして、お礼を言っている。
Asking other humans for favors and saying thank you...

393
00:36:06,056 --> 00:36:06,556
うん。
Yeah.

394
00:36:06,256 --> 00:36:08,556
自分でやった方が早いだろうに。
It'd be faster if you just did it yourself.

395
00:36:08,606 --> 00:36:12,206
クマは自分で全部やるから、お礼なんて言う必要がない。
Bears do everything themselves, so there's no need to say thank you.

396
00:36:12,556 --> 00:36:13,656
孤独。
So lonely.

397
00:36:13,756 --> 00:36:16,756
今日のフォルスタッフの人間入門はここまで。
That's all for today's Falstaff's Intro to Humans.

398
00:36:14,756 --> 00:36:15,756
孤独...
Lonely...

399
00:36:15,956 --> 00:36:17,256
孤独ベアー。
Lonely bear.

400
00:36:16,806 --> 00:36:17,906
じゃあまた。
See you next time.

401
00:36:17,956 --> 00:36:21,656
Alone... lonely... lonely bear!

402
00:36:22,056 --> 00:36:23,756
Lonely bear!

403
00:36:24,356 --> 00:36:28,556
冷笑ロンリーベアー。だから... だから孤独なんじゃ。
Cynical lonely bear. That's why... that's why he's lonely.

404
00:36:29,356 --> 00:36:31,856
うん... bear...
Yeah... bear...

405
00:36:32,056 --> 00:36:41,556
フォルスタッフ said あの... "I did all things with myself" って...
Falstaff said, um... "I did all things with myself"...

406
00:36:41,556 --> 00:36:44,556
って言ってた。1人でやった方が早いって言ってた。
He said that. He said it's faster to do it alone.

407
00:36:44,856 --> 00:36:47,556
I did well...

408
00:36:48,856 --> 00:36:57,256
done... ask another people... うん。
done... ask another people... yeah.

409
00:36:57,656 --> 00:36:59,756
って言ってたのじゃ。
That's what he was saying.

410
00:37:00,056 --> 00:37:03,756
うん。孤独だから言葉に協調性なくてもいいってこと。
Yeah. Because he's lonely, he doesn't have to speak cooperatively.

411
00:37:03,856 --> 00:37:16,356
いや、わしもね！わしもね、かつて孤独だったから言葉に協調性がなくても許されてきたんじゃけど、ホロライブに入ってしまった以上、協調性がないとちょっと嫌な思いをさせてしまうらしいから気をつけたい気持ちあり。
Man, for me too! I used to be lonely too, so people let it slide even if my words weren't cooperative, but now that I've joined Hololive, it seems not being cooperative might make people feel bad, so I want to be careful.

412
00:37:16,556 --> 00:37:22,056
孤独歴が長すぎて... 孤独歴が長すぎてコミュニケーションがガチで分からんのじゃ。
